// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): every
// potentially-evaluated call to an immediate function shall produce a
// constant expression. A non-type template argument must be a converted
// constant expression ([temp.arg.nontype]), so this is another position that
// no runtime evaluation could satisfy: the argument is consumed during
// template argument substitution, before any code exists.
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support the call. Non-type template parameters of scalar
// type are the language surface exercised by
// 21_generic_types/non_type_template_parameter_scalar.scpp (ch05 §5.14).
//
// Call position: template argument. Callee: free `consteval` function.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS, and for a reason that is *not*
// consteval-specific: scpp reports "error: unsupported non-type template
// argument expression (ch05 §5.14 only supports an integer literal, a bare
// parameter name, or a '+' of the two)". No clause of docs/spec/ restricts a
// non-type template argument that way, so front-matter §1(2) leaves
// [temp.arg.nontype]'s converted-constant-expression rule in force. This cell
// therefore needs both the general non-type-template-argument restriction
// lifted and immediate-call folding to work. The expectation above comes from
// the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the implementation.
consteval int twice(int x) {
    return x * 2;
}

template<int N>
class FixedValue {
public:
    virtual ~FixedValue() { return; }
public:
    int get_n() {
        return N;
    }
};

int main() {
    FixedValue<twice(21)> v{};
    return v.get_n();
}
